  "prediction": "**Acute Flaccid Myelitis (AFM) Outbreak: A Growing Concern**\n\nA mysterious polio-like illness, known as Acute Flaccid Myelitis (AFM), has been on the rise in the United States, with a significant increase in reported cases this year. According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), there have been 32 new cases of AFM confirmed across the United States through July, a sharp rise compared to last year, when just seven cases had been confirmed by that month.\n\n**What is AFM?**\n\nAFM is a rare condition that affects the nervous system, specifically the spinal cord. It is characterized by sudden onset of muscle weakness, often in the arms and legs, and can lead to respiratory failure. The exact cause of AFM is unknown, but it has been linked to viral infections, including enteroviruses, adenoviruses, and the mosquito-borne West Nile virus.\n\n**Symptoms and Diagnosis**\n\nSymptoms of AFM include pain in the arms and legs, an inability to pass urine, and respiratory failure. The condition is often diagnosed through a combination of clinical evaluation, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and laboratory tests. However, there is no specific treatment for AFM, and most cases are treated with physical therapy and supportive care.\n\n**Outbreaks and Cases**\n\nThe CDC reported 50 cases of AFM across 24 states from January 1 to August 31, 2016, compared to 21 cases in 2015. In 2014, there were 120 cases reported between August and December, all of which were children. The average age of children affected by AFM is 7, and most have had to be placed on respirators.\n\n**Investigation and Prevention**\n\nThe CDC is investigating the current rise of AFM cases, and health officials are advising people to wash their hands with soap and water, avoid close contact with sick people, and clean surfaces with a disinfectant. The CDC is also collaborating with partners nationally to investigate reported cases, risk factors, and possible etiologies of this condition.\n\n**Personal Stories**\n\nSeveral families have shared their personal stories of dealing with AFM. One family, the Roberts, had their 3-year-old son, Carter, diagnosed with AFM in July. Carter can now only move the left side of his face and wiggle a toe. Another family, the Carsons, had their 4-year-old daughter, Laura, diagnosed with AFM in",
